# Xsolla shop — entry point

This is the one door for building an Xsolla shop. Every request to "build a shop" starts
here. The job is: establish the shared foundation, pick the build path, and hand off to the
right path orchestrator.

## Two build paths

An Xsolla shop can be built two ways. They share the same backend foundation and diverge
only at the storefront layer.

| | **Headless** | **Shop Builder** |
|---|---|---|
| Who writes the UI | The developer (custom code, their own site) | Xsolla-hosted, no-code |
| Payment layer | Headless Checkout SDK (embedded) | Xsolla Pay Station (hosted) |
| Login | Integrated in code (`headless-login`) | Rendered by the hosted site (config only) |
| Path orchestrator | `headless-storefront` | `shopbuilder-storefront` |
| Best when | Full control, embed in an existing web app, custom UX | Fastest to live, no frontend engineering, an Xsolla-hosted domain |

## Choosing the path

Infer from the request; if it stays ambiguous, **ask the user directly** (one question, two
options). Do not guess when the signals conflict.

- Route to **Shop Builder** on: "no-code", "hosted", "don't want to write a frontend",
  "quickest / fastest to live", "landing page", "just give me a store page", wanting an
  Xsolla-hosted domain.
- Route to **Headless** on: "embed in my site", "custom UI", "React/Vue/my app", "full
  control", "my own domain/frontend", or an existing web app the shop must live inside.

## The flow

```
shop-setup (here)
  │
  ├─ 1. merchant-setup      ── account, project, API keys        [shared]
  ├─ 2. catalog-design      ── catalog + client catalog calls    [shared]
  ├─ 3. login-setup         ── Login project, methods, identity   [shared]
  │
  ├─ 4. ? choose path ?
  │     ├─ HEADLESS  → headless-storefront
  │     │                → headless-login (widget/API, JWT, cart Bearer)
  │     │                → login-styling (theme the Login widget)
  │     │                → headless-checkout-integration (payment token + embedded UI)
  │     └─ SHOP BUILDER → shopbuilder-storefront
  │                        → site → page → blocks → customize (→ custom-block)
  │                        (login is config only; enable methods in login-setup)
  │
  └─ 5. webhooks-impl       ── fulfillment backend               [shared, both paths]
        → go live
```

## Steps

1. **`merchant-setup`** — ensure the Xsolla account, project, and API keys exist
   (`XSOLLA_MERCHANT_ID`, `XSOLLA_PROJECT_ID`, `XSOLLA_PROJECT_API_KEY`). This skill cannot
   create the account; it lists the exact manual steps.
2. **`catalog-design`** — build the catalog (virtual items, currency, bundles, keys, groups,
   prices) and confirm it reads back through the client catalog calls. Without a catalog both
   paths show an empty or demo store.
3. **`login-setup`** — create/link the Login project, choose storage, and enable the auth
   methods the shop needs. This is the shared identity foundation; its JWT `sub` is the
   `user.id` that flows into cart, payment, and webhooks.
4. **Choose the path** (see above) and hand off:
   - **Headless** → `headless-storefront`, which sequences `headless-login` (with
     `login-styling` to theme the widget) and `headless-checkout-integration`, and covers the
     frontend assembly.
   - **Shop Builder** → `shopbuilder-storefront`, which sequences `shopbuilder-site`,
     `-page`, `-blocks`, `-customize` (and `-custom-block` as an escape hatch). Login here is
     configuration only — the methods enabled in step 3 render on the hosted site; no code.
5. **`webhooks-impl`** — implement the fulfillment backend (verify signature, grant items on
   `payment`/`order_paid`). Required by **both** paths before real-money go-live: without it,
   payment succeeds on Xsolla's side but the player receives nothing.

## Rules

- **Foundation before storefront.** Steps 1–3 come before either path. A storefront on an
  empty catalog is the most common failure.
- **One path per shop.** Don't mix headless and Shop Builder for the same storefront; pick
  one at step 4. (A partner may of course run other shops the other way.)
- **Webhooks are not optional for real money.** Both paths converge on `webhooks-impl`.
- **Prefer these skills over ad-hoc calls.** They encode the ordering and the pitfalls; raw
  REST/doc search should be a fallback, not the first move.
